  "query_preview": "..(Thursday through Tuesday)\nIssued at 437 PM EDT TUE JUL 28 2026\n\nThe long-term period opens Thursday morning with a deep trough axis\nextending from Quebec southward to along the Mid-Atlantic and\nSoutheast US coast. Meanwhile, an ~596 dam high is in place over \nthe Southern Rockies with broader ridging across much of the \nCentral and Western CONUS. This leaves the Ohio Valley in a zone \nof subsidence on the eastern side of the ridging, corresponding to\nan area of surface high pressure. There is also a shortwave \ntrough riding over the top of the ridging across the Dakotas at \nthe start of the period.\n\nAside from some nighttime and morning fog in the typically favored\nriver valley locales, fair weather will be found across eastern\nKentucky through Friday as surface high pressure crests over and\njust northeast of the JKL CWA. This will keep relatively cool\ntemperatures in place for both Thursday and Friday with highs in\nthe lower to mid 80s and lows in the mid 50s to near 60F. Dew \npoints falling into the upper 50s to lower 60s will make it feel \nquite comfortable.\n\nLooking ahead to the weekend and early next week, the shortwave\ntrough over the Northern Plains will dig southeastward and close\noff into a low over the Mid-Mississippi Valley. A weak area of\nsurface low pressure will develop ahead of this feature over the\nCentral Plains on Thursday night into Friday, then occlude and\ndecay near or over the Commonwealth early next week. This system \nwill wrap higher humidity levels back into eastern Kentucky while\nalso bringing rounds of convection. As a result, daily high \ntemperatures will remain on the cool side, generally in the lower \nto middle 80s, while nighttime lows moderate to between 65 and \n70F.",
